git status\subsection{Model Validation and Design Implications}
\label{sec:model_validation}

The latency models provide a framework for understanding the SSD bottleneck and identifying the system-level tradeoffs that determine achievable throughput. They are not used during runtime prediction or scheduling; instead, they guide the selection of operating points for predictive prefetching and cache-conditional routing. Specifically, the models characterize the impact of expert cache capacity, SSD latency, compute latency, prediction stride, prefetch budget, prediction precision and recall, and cache-conditional routing parameters.

To evaluate the accuracy of the analytical models, we compare their predicted throughput against measured throughput under three scenarios: baseline SSD-backed decode, predictive prefetching with an oracle predictor, and cache-conditional routing. These experiments are performed on a system with sufficient unified memory to hold the entire model, enabling controlled sweeps of expert cache capacity from fully SSD-streamed inference ($C=0$) to the no-offloading upper bound where all experts remain resident in memory. This setup isolates the impact of expert movement predicted by the analytical models, while the end-to-end system evaluation is performed on the memory-constrained AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX 370 platform.

Figure~\ref{fig:model_validation} shows the predicted and measured throughput for the baseline SSD-backed decode model as cache capacity increases. The analytical model captures the dominant throughput trend, with deviations resulting from practical runtime effects not represented in the abstraction, including SSD scheduling overhead and interactions between memory transfers and computation. The predictive prefetching model extends this analysis by estimating the number of SSD loads that can be hidden through asynchronous prefetching. This model captures the tradeoff between prediction horizon and SSD latency hiding: increasing the prediction stride increases the available overlap window, but also increases the number of future experts that must be predicted and stored. Similarly, prediction precision, recall, and prefetch budget determine how many expert loads can be successfully hidden. These relationships motivate the prediction configurations evaluated experimentally using the oracle predictor.

The cache-conditional model similarly characterizes the tradeoff between reducing SSD traffic and preserving model quality. Increasing the number of preserved top-$J$ experts reduces the number of possible substitutions but limits the potential reduction in SSD loads. The measured results follow the trends predicted by the analytical model, demonstrating that the models capture the primary factors governing SSD-backed MoE inference throughput. Together, these models establish the design space explored by ExpertAhead and ExpertAhead-CC and provide insight into the limitations and opportunities of predictive prefetching and cache-conditional routing.

